{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_text": "Transaction IDs are only 32 bits wide.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly states that transaction IDs have limited size (32 bits).",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "But since transaction IDs have limited size (32 bits) a cluster that runs for a long time (more than 4 billion transactions) would suffer transaction ID wraparound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The XID space holds about 4 billion values.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage connects 32-bit transaction IDs with wraparound after more than 4 billion transactions, which entails an XID space of about 4 billion values.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "But since transaction IDs have limited size (32 bits) a cluster that runs for a long time (more than 4 billion transactions) would suffer transaction ID wraparound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Every normal XID has roughly two billion older and two billion newer XIDs.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage states exactly this comparison property of normal XIDs.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "This means that for every normal XID, there are two billion XIDs that are “older” and two billion that are “newer”"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Every table must be vacuumed at least once every two billion transactions.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage directly states this requirement.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "it is necessary to vacuum every table in every database at least once every two billion transactions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The two-billion-transaction vacuum requirement is intended to avoid transaction ID wraparound.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explains that wraparound would occur and that vacuuming is necessary to avoid it.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "a cluster that runs for a long time (more than 4 billion transactions) would suffer transaction ID wraparound"
        },
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "To avoid this, it is necessary to vacuum every table in every database at least once every two billion transactions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Running past the two-billion-transaction limit without vacuuming can cause data loss.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages describe catastrophic data loss from wraparound and say data loss could result if a table goes unvacuumed too long.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "In short, catastrophic data loss."
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "If it were to go unvacuumed for longer than that, data loss could result."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The data loss caused by running past the limit without vacuuming is permanent and unrecoverable.",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages say the data loss is catastrophic and that the data is still there but inaccessible, but they do not state that it is permanent or unrecoverable.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The default autovacuum_freeze_max_age is 200 million transactions.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage discusses autovacuum_freeze_max_age and then gives its default as 200 million transactions.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "The commit status uses two bits per transaction, so if autovacuum_freeze_max_age is set to its maximum allowed value of two billion, pg_xact can be expected to grow to about half a gigabyte and pg_commit_ts to about 20GB. If this is trivial compared to your total database size, setting autovacuum_freeze_max_age to its maximum allowed value is recommended. Otherwise, set it depending on what you are willing to allow for pg_xact and pg_commit_ts storage. (The default, 200 million transactions, translates to about 50MB of pg_xact storage and about 2GB of pg_commit_ts storage.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Autovacuum is invoked on tables that might contain unfrozen rows with XIDs older than autovacuum_freeze_max_age.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage directly states this invocation condition for autovacuum.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "autovacuum is invoked on any table that might contain unfrozen rows with XIDs older than the age specified by the configuration parameter autovacuum_freeze_max_age."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "With the default autovacuum_freeze_max_age of 200 million transactions, the anti-wraparound autovacuum kicks in after about 10% of the two-billion-transaction window.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages state that anti-wraparound autovacuum is triggered at autovacuum_freeze_max_age, that the default is 200 million, and that the vacuum window is two billion transactions; 200 million is about 10% of two billion.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "autovacuum is invoked on any table that might contain unfrozen rows with XIDs older than the age specified by the configuration parameter autovacuum_freeze_max_age."
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "A value higher than autovacuum_freeze_max_age wouldn't make sense because an anti-wraparound autovacuum would be triggered at that point anyway"
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "The commit status uses two bits per transaction, so if autovacuum_freeze_max_age is set to its maximum allowed value of two billion, pg_xact can be expected to grow to about half a gigabyte and pg_commit_ts to about 20GB. If this is trivial compared to your total database size, setting autovacuum_freeze_max_age to its maximum allowed value is recommended. Otherwise, set it depending on what you are willing to allow for pg_xact and pg_commit_ts storage. (The default, 200 million transactions, translates to about 50MB of pg_xact storage and about 2GB of pg_commit_ts storage.)"
        },
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "it is necessary to vacuum every table in every database at least once every two billion transactions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The effective maximum for vacuum_freeze_table_age is 95% of autovacuum_freeze_max_age.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage states this effective maximum explicitly.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "The effective maximum for vacuum_freeze_table_age is 0.95 * autovacuum_freeze_max_age; a setting higher than that will be capped to the maximum."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "With the default autovacuum_freeze_max_age, the effective maximum for vacuum_freeze_table_age is 180 million.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages state that the effective maximum is 0.95 times autovacuum_freeze_max_age and that the default autovacuum_freeze_max_age is 200 million; 0.95 * 200 million is 190 million, not 180 million.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "The effective maximum for vacuum_freeze_table_age is 0.95 * autovacuum_freeze_max_age; a setting higher than that will be capped to the maximum."
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "The commit status uses two bits per transaction, so if autovacuum_freeze_max_age is set to its maximum allowed value of two billion, pg_xact can be expected to grow to about half a gigabyte and pg_commit_ts to about 20GB. If this is trivial compared to your total database size, setting autovacuum_freeze_max_age to its maximum allowed value is recommended. Otherwise, set it depending on what you are willing to allow for pg_xact and pg_commit_ts storage. (The default, 200 million transactions, translates to about 50MB of pg_xact storage and about 2GB of pg_commit_ts storage.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "If autovacuum fails, warnings start when the oldest XIDs are forty million transactions from wraparound.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage directly states this warning threshold.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "If for some reason autovacuum fails to clear old XIDs from a table, the system will begin to emit warning messages like this when the database's oldest XIDs reach forty million transactions from the wraparound point:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "New XIDs are refused once fewer than three million transactions remain until wraparound.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage directly states this refusal threshold.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 7,
          "quote": "the system will refuse to assign new XIDs once there are fewer than three million transactions left until wraparound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "There is a 37-million-transaction window between the first warning and the hard stop.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages give warning at forty million transactions before wraparound and refusal at fewer than three million transactions before wraparound; the difference is about 37 million transactions.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "If for some reason autovacuum fails to clear old XIDs from a table, the system will begin to emit warning messages like this when the database's oldest XIDs reach forty million transactions from the wraparound point:"
        },
        {
          "index": 7,
          "quote": "the system will refuse to assign new XIDs once there are fewer than three million transactions left until wraparound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Once XID assignment stops, no new transactions of any kind can be started.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says that in this condition read-only transactions can still be started and VACUUM can still be run, so it is not true that no new transactions of any kind can be started.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 8,
          "quote": "In this condition any transactions already in progress can continue, but only read-only transactions can be started."
        },
        {
          "index": 8,
          "quote": "The VACUUM command can still be run normally."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_text": "Autovacuum relies on the statistics collection facility.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage states that autovacuum's checks use the statistics collection facility.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "When enabled, autovacuum checks for tables that have had a large number of inserted, updated or deleted tuples. These checks use the statistics collection facility; therefore, autovacuum cannot be used unless track_counts is set to true."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "If track_counts is off, autovacuum cannot be used.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says autovacuum cannot be used unless track_counts is true, which entails it cannot be used when track_counts is off.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "autovacuum cannot be used unless track_counts is set to true."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Turning track_counts on is by itself enough for autovacuum to work.",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages state that track_counts is necessary for autovacuum, but they do not state that enabling track_counts alone is sufficient for autovacuum to work.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Even when autovacuum is disabled, PostgreSQL still invokes it on tables that might contain unfrozen rows with XIDs older than autovacuum_freeze_max_age.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly says autovacuum is invoked on such tables even if autovacuum is disabled.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "autovacuum is invoked on any table that might contain unfrozen rows with XIDs older than the age specified by the configuration parameter autovacuum_freeze_max_age. (This will happen even if autovacuum is disabled.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The launcher tries to start one worker per database every autovacuum_naptime seconds.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage states that the launcher attempts to start one worker within each database every autovacuum_naptime seconds.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "The launcher will distribute the work across time, attempting to start one worker within each database every autovacuum_naptime seconds."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "With 4 databases and autovacuum_naptime set to 60 seconds, a new worker starts about every 15 seconds.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage gives the formula that a new worker is launched every autovacuum_naptime/N seconds; with N=4 and autovacuum_naptime=60, that is 15 seconds.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "(Therefore, if the installation has N databases, a new worker will be launched every autovacuum_naptime/N seconds.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Each database gets at most one worker at a time.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says there is no limit on how many workers might be in a single database, which contradicts the claim that each database gets at most one worker at a time.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "There is no limit on how many workers might be in a single database"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Running workers count against max_connections.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly says running autovacuum workers do not count toward max_connections.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "Note that the number of running workers does not count towards max_connections or superuser_reserved_connections limits."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Autovacuum processes partitions like ordinary tables.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage states that autovacuum processes table partitions just like other tables.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "(Autovacuum does process table partitions just like other tables.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Autovacuum does not run ANALYZE on partitioned tables.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly says autovacuum does not run ANALYZE on partitioned tables.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "Unfortunately, this means that autovacuum does not run ANALYZE on partitioned tables"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Autovacuum does not issue ANALYZE for foreign tables.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly says the autovacuum daemon does not issue ANALYZE commands for foreign tables.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 7,
          "quote": "The autovacuum daemon does not issue ANALYZE commands for foreign tables"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Normally a conflicting lock request interrupts autovacuum.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says that acquisition of a conflicting lock interrupts autovacuum.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "If a process attempts to acquire a lock that conflicts with the SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE lock held by autovacuum, lock acquisition will interrupt the autovacuum."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "A conflicting lock request interrupts an autovacuum that is running to prevent transaction ID wraparound.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says that an autovacuum running to prevent transaction ID wraparound is not automatically interrupted.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "However, if the autovacuum is running to prevent transaction ID wraparound (i.e., the autovacuum query name in the pg_stat_activity view ends with (to prevent wraparound)), the autovacuum is not automatically interrupted."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_text": "Standard VACUUM can run alongside normal SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE traffic.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage states that standard VACUUM can run in parallel with production operations and that SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE continue to function normally.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "Also, the standard form of VACUUM can run in parallel with production database operations. (Commands such as SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE will continue to function normally, though you will not be able to modify the definition of a table with commands such as ALTER TABLE while it is being vacuumed.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "VACUUM FULL needs an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock on the table.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly says VACUUM FULL requires an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock on the table it is working on.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "VACUUM FULL requires an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock on the table it is working on"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "VACUUM FULL is the faster of the two VACUUM variants.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says VACUUM FULL runs much more slowly than standard VACUUM.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "VACUUM FULL can reclaim more disk space but runs much more slowly."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "VACUUM FULL compacts the table.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage states that VACUUM FULL actively compacts tables by writing a complete new version of the table file with no dead space.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "In contrast, VACUUM FULL actively compacts tables by writing a complete new version of the table file with no dead space."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "VACUUM FULL compacts the table in a single pass.",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages describe VACUUM FULL as writing a complete new version of the table file, but they do not state that this happens in a single pass.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Standard VACUUM never returns space to the operating system.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says standard VACUUM generally does not return space to the operating system, but it provides an exception where pages at the end of a table become entirely free.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "However, it will not return the space to the operating system, except in the special case where one or more pages at the end of a table become entirely free and an exclusive table lock can be easily obtained."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "VACUUM FULL, CLUSTER and the table-rewriting forms of ALTER TABLE all temporarily need extra disk space roughly equal to the size of the table.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage lists VACUUM FULL, CLUSTER, and table-rewriting ALTER TABLE variants and says they temporarily use extra disk space approximately equal to the size of the table.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "you will need to use VACUUM FULL, or alternatively CLUSTER or one of the table-rewriting variants of ALTER TABLE. These commands rewrite an entire new copy of the table and build new indexes for it. All these options require an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock. Note that they also temporarily use extra disk space approximately equal to the size of the table"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Rewriting a 50 GB table needs about another 50 GB free.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says these rewriting operations temporarily use extra disk space approximately equal to the size of the table, which entails about 50 GB extra for a 50 GB table.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "Note that they also temporarily use extra disk space approximately equal to the size of the table"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The autovacuum daemon never issues VACUUM FULL.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly states that the autovacuum daemon will never issue VACUUM FULL.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "The autovacuum daemon attempts to work this way, and in fact will never issue VACUUM FULL."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "A VACUUM FULL that shows up in your logs was not started by autovacuum.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Because the passage says the autovacuum daemon never issues VACUUM FULL, any VACUUM FULL observed was not started by autovacuum.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "The autovacuum daemon attempts to work this way, and in fact will never issue VACUUM FULL."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "For a table whose entire contents are deleted periodically, TRUNCATE avoids the need for a follow-up VACUUM.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says TRUNCATE removes the entire table immediately and does not require a subsequent VACUUM or VACUUM FULL to reclaim the space.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 7,
          "quote": "If you have a table whose entire contents are deleted on a periodic basis, consider doing it with TRUNCATE rather than using DELETE followed by VACUUM. TRUNCATE removes the entire content of the table immediately, without requiring a subsequent VACUUM or VACUUM FULL to reclaim the now-unused disk space."
        }
